out.push(S('Stage 4R · Report – mediation (sample)'));
out.push(SAMPLE(['Mrs Khoury said there are two letters and the first is one line: the clerk gets a thank-you that names an act, because she asked for the declaration, which nobody outside her section knew existed, and then the section head gets the real letter, which is about publishing the 2017 list and is written for the next applicant. Mr Tayara said what must not be said is that she could have told him in February, and that is exactly it and it is true, which is the whole difficulty: she could have told him and she did not, because he had never asked, and a letter that points that out has turned a two-line thank-you into a complaint about the one person who ended three months of difficulty in six days. Mrs Oueidat asked whether anybody is at fault, and Mr Attar said nobody, and this is the fifth time in Book 2 and the clearest: the applicant did not know the list existed, the clerk was never asked, the rule says nothing about declarations, and the list is eight years old and does what everybody needed. Mrs Rizk asked who can close the gap, and Mrs Khoury said the person who can publish the list, and that is a section head and not a clerk, so the second letter goes there and asks for one thing. Dr Qabbani asked why the letter should say first that his own file is closed, and Mrs Oueidat said because a letter from a man whose file is open is read as a continuation of his case however it is worded. Mr Shihabi asked whether a letter like that ever works, and sometimes, and slowly, and that is not the reason to send it: the reason is that the next applicant is a head of school who has not read this unit. Mrs Tarabishi said that is an uncomfortable sentence, and Mrs Oueidat said it is the finding of the unit and it is about us and not about the directorate, because every move in these seven cycles is a way of getting your own file through a system and the last of them is the only one that costs you something after your file has gone through. Mrs Ajouz asked whether the letter should name the list, and it names it exactly, with its date and the clause he was given under it, and it says where he learnt of it. Mr Zahreddine asked about the thanks, and one line, and it names the act. And Mr Tayara said the thing he will take away is the eight years: a list that answers his question has been in that section since 2017, and what he had been doing for three months instead was reading a regulation very carefully, which is what an intelligent person does when they have not thought to ask a question.']));
out.push(A('⚠ **Three lines to carry out of the unit, in this order.** **First: nobody is at fault, and the room must say it about each of the four accounts.** **Second: the gap can be closed only by somebody with the power to publish, so the second letter goes to a section head and says in its first line that the writer\'s own file is closed.** **Third, and last: the eight years.** **A list that answered the question in six days has been in that section since 2017** — **and what Mr Tayara had been doing for three months instead was reading a regulation very carefully, which is what an intelligent person does when they have not thought to ask a question.**'));
out.push(S('Board clinic – typical mistakes'));
out.push(A('**1** ⚠ _She could have told me in February_ → **true, and a complaint about the person who helped you.**   **2** **One letter instead of two** → **the thanks and the request do different work and must not share an envelope.**   **3** ⚠ **The writer\'s own file left open** → **the letter is read as a continuation of his case.**   **4** **The internal list reported without saying how you learnt of it** → **the clerk becomes a source.**   **5** ⚠ **Thanks of more than one line** → **it becomes about the writer\'s gratitude, which cannot be filed.**   **6** **A request for the next applicant dressed as a request for yourself** → **it will be answered as your case and refused.**'));
